---
export const prerender = true

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCard from "../../components/docs/DocCard.astro";
import DocCode from "../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "Overview", href: "#overview", depth: 2 },
  { title: "What Brain does", href: "#what", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Trigger conditions", href: "#trigger", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Configuration", href: "#config", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Memory file", href: "#memory", depth: 2 },
  { title: "TUI & API surface", href: "#surface", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Source anchors", href: "#source", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Brain"
  subtitle="The Brain subsystem periodically consolidates session memory and re-evaluates long-running context — TUI plugin, server route, and headless scheduler."
  toc={toc}
  description="How nikcli's Brain subsystem schedules, triggers, and runs memory consolidation sessions, with its config keys, thresholds, and TUI surface."
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is nikcli's "long-term memory" subsystem. While a session is running, every
      tool call, message, and decision lives in the session's JSON store; Brain's job is
      to periodically consolidate that signal into a single, well-organised
      <code>memory.instruction.md</code> file that future coding sessions can read at
      startup to orient quickly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Brain is implemented in <code>packages/nikcli/src/brain/</code> and surfaces in three
      places: a headless scheduler that ticks every hour, a TUI feature plugin for the
      <code>brain</code> model picker, and a server route (<code>/brain</code>) plus a
      typed <code>httpapi/brain.ts</code> group for the Studio dashboard and external
      clients.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Experimental feature flag">
      Brain is enabled by default but is part of the <code>experimental</code> config
      group. Disable it globally with <code>experimental.brain = false</code>, or turn off
      just the memory write with <code>experimental.memory = false</code>.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What Brain does</h2>
    <p>
      When triggered, Brain runs a real nikcli session under the hood — but a session with
      a very narrow scope. It:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Reads the existing memory file (if any) under
        <code>.github/instructions/memory.instruction.md</code>.</li>
      <li>Pulls the most recent N sessions (default 10) and formats their message parts
        into a compact review.</li>
      <li>Sends the prompt
        (<code>Brain.buildBrainPrompt(memoryPath, reviews, currentMemory)</code>) to a
        dedicated session titled <code>BRAIN_SESSION_TITLE</code>, with permissions
        restricted to read/edit-only — no <code>task</code>, no <code>todo</code>, no
        <code>bash</code>.</li>
      <li>Cancels the session via <code>prompt.cancel</code> after
        <code>BRAIN_SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS = 5 minutes</code>.</li>
      <li>Diff-checks the memory file before/after. If it changed, marks the run as a
        success and writes the brain lock file (<code>Global.Path.state/.brain-lock</code>)
        so the next <code>shouldTrigger</code> check has a fresh anchor.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The consolidating model is configurable through <code>experimental.brainModel</code>
      (<code>"provider/model"</code>). When the configured model is unknown or unreachable,
      Brain silently falls back to the user's default model so the pass still runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="trigger" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Trigger conditions</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Brain.shouldTrigger()</code> decides whether the scheduler should fire. It
      refuses to run when Brain or memory is disabled, when the cooldown has not elapsed,
      when a scan has happened in the last <code>SCAN_THROTTLE_MS = 10 minutes</code>, or
      when there aren't enough sessions since the last successful Brain pass.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Constant</th>
          <th>Default</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>DEFAULTS.minHours</code></td>
          <td><code>24</code></td>
          <td>Minimum hours between successful Brain passes (configurable via <code>experimental.brainMinHours</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>DEFAULTS.minSessions</code></td>
          <td><code>5</code></td>
          <td>Minimum new sessions since the last pass before Brain fires (configurable via <code>experimental.brainMinSessions</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>SCAN_THROTTLE_MS</code></td>
          <td><code>10 * 60 * 1000</code></td>
          <td>How often <code>shouldTrigger()</code> is allowed to scan session history.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>LOCK_DURATION_MS</code></td>
          <td><code>1 hour</code></td>
          <td>Stale-lease duration for the <code>brain</code> flock lock.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>BRAIN_SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS</code></td>
          <td><code>5 minutes</code></td>
          <td>Hard ceiling on a single Brain session; cancelled via <code>prompt.cancel</code> on expiry.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>SESSION_REVIEW_LIMIT</code></td>
          <td><code>10</code></td>
          <td>How many recent sessions are included in the prompt.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>SESSION_REVIEW_MAX_CHARS</code></td>
          <td><code>12_000</code></td>
          <td>Per-session review truncation length.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The scheduler registers itself with
      <code>Scheduler.register(&#123; id: "brain", interval: 60 * 60 * 1000, scope: "instance" &#125;)</code>
      — one tick per hour, scoped to the project instance.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Force runs bypass the throttle">
      <code>Brain.trigger(&#123; force: true &#125;)</code> skips the
      <code>shouldTrigger</code> gate (except for the <code>enabled</code> and
      <code>memoryEnabled</code> checks) and reviews the most recent sessions even when
      no time has elapsed. The TUI dialog and the <code>/brain/trigger</code> route expose
      this path.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="config" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Configuration</h2>
    <p>
      All Brain knobs live under <code>experimental</code> in
      <code>nikcli.json</code>:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="json" title="nikcli.json">{`{
  "experimental": {
    "brain": true,
    "memory": true,
    "brainMinHours": 24,
    "brainMinSessions": 5,
    "brainModel": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5"
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Default</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>experimental.brain</code></td>
          <td><code>true</code></td>
          <td>Master switch for the Brain subsystem. When <code>false</code>, neither the scheduler nor a manual trigger will fire.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>experimental.memory</code></td>
          <td><code>true</code></td>
          <td>Whether Brain is allowed to write the memory file. Turning this off keeps Brain's read path active but disables consolidation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>experimental.brainMinHours</code></td>
          <td><code>24</code></td>
          <td>Override <code>DEFAULTS.minHours</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>experimental.brainMinSessions</code></td>
          <td><code>5</code></td>
          <td>Override <code>DEFAULTS.minSessions</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>experimental.brainModel</code></td>
          <td>(default provider/model)</td>
          <td>"provider/model" string the consolidation session runs on. Falls back silently to the user's default model when unavailable.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="memory" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Memory file</h2>
    <p>
      Brain writes to <code>.github/instructions/memory.instruction.md</code> inside the
      project directory (created on demand). The path is the same convention used by
      GitHub Copilot for project-level instructions, so the file is shared automatically
      with anything in the repo that reads <code>.github/instructions/*.md</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The consolidating prompt focuses on four phases — orient, gather, consolidate,
      prune — and explicitly forbids creating a new memory file or asking the user
      questions. The full prompt template is in <code>Brain.buildBrainPrompt()</code>; the
      resulting memory file is meant to be concise, dated, and indexed for fast lookup.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="success" title="Brain is also a long-term signal for other agents">
      Because the file lives under <code>.github/instructions/</code>, every future
      nikcli session in the same project picks it up automatically — it becomes part of
      the system prompt context for the next agent run.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="surface" class="doc-section">
    <h2>TUI & API surface</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is reachable from three places: the scheduler (passive), the TUI dialog
      (<code>dialog-brain.tsx</code> — pick the model and force a run), and the server
      routes.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="TUI dialog" eyebrow="dialog-brain.tsx">
        <p>
          Bound to the <code>brain</code> feature plugin and surfaced from the command
          palette. Lets you pick the consolidation model and trigger a forced run.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Server routes" eyebrow="server/routes/brain.ts">
        <p>
          <code>GET /brain</code> returns the full status payload (enabled, memory enabled,
          min hours, min sessions, last brain at, hours since, sessions since, should
          trigger, configured model). <code>POST /brain/trigger</code> accepts
          <code>&#123;force: boolean&#125;</code> and runs <code>Brain.trigger</code>.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Typed HttpApi" eyebrow="server/httpapi/brain.ts">
        <p>
          Same surface under the typed HttpApi group
          (<code>operationId: brain.status</code>, <code>brain.trigger</code>) — mounted
          through <code>HttpApiBridge</code> when <code>NIKCLI_EXPERIMENTAL_HTTPAPI</code> is
          set. Used by the Studio dashboard and SDK clients.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Headless scheduler">
      The scheduler registers itself from <code>initBrainScheduler()</code> with
      <code>scope: "instance"</code>. The scheduler definition is in
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/scheduler/</code>; one tick per hour, per project
      instance.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="source" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Source anchors</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Concern</th>
          <th>File</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Core service + consolidation prompt</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/brain/index.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hourly scheduler</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/brain/scheduler.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Constants</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/brain/constants.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Classic routes</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/server/routes/brain.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Typed HttpApi</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/server/httpapi/brain.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>TUI plugin</td>
          <td>
            <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/feature-plugins/brain/index.tsx</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>TUI dialog</td>
          <td><code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/component/dialog-brain.tsx</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>